.TH TELNETD 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
telnetd \- TELNET protocol server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B telnetd
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR login-program ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B telnetd
is a stand-alone TELNET server.
It listens on TCP port 23 on all IPv4 addresses (with
.BR SO_REUSEADDR ),
and serves each connection in a separate thread of the same process.
.PP
For each connection it allocates a pseudo-terminal with
.BR openpty (3),
sends
.BR "IAC WILL ECHO" ,
.B "IAC WILL SUPPRESS-GO-AHEAD"
and
.BR "IAC DO NAWS" ,
and forks a child that makes the pty slave its controlling terminal and
standard input, output and error with
.BR login_tty (3)
and executes the login program, with
.B login
as
.I argv[0]
and no other arguments.
.PP
The thread then copies data between the connection and the pty master
until either side closes, then closes both and waits for the child.
From the network, TELNET commands
.RB ( IAC
sequences) and subnegotiations are discarded, a doubled
.B IAC
becomes one 0xff byte, and a carriage return followed by NUL or LF becomes
a single carriage return.
Towards the network, each 0xff byte is doubled.
No option is negotiated beyond the initial three; the window size reported
by the client is ignored.
.PP
Unless
.B \-f
is given,
.B telnetd
detaches with
.BR daemon (3)
after binding the socket.
It then writes its pid to
.IR /var/run/telnetd.pid ,
ignores
.BR SIGPIPE ,
and accepts connections until
.BR accept (2)
fails.
If a thread cannot be created, the connection is closed.
.PP
It is started at boot by
.IR /etc/rc.d/35\-telnetd ;
the telnet entry in
.I /etc/inetd.conf
is disabled in favour of it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage message and exit 0.
.TP
.BI \-l " login-program"
Execute
.I login-program
for each session instead of
.IR /bin/login .
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Listen on
.I port
instead of 23.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B \-h
was given.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a failure to create, bind or listen on the socket, a
failure to detach, or a failed
.BR accept (2)
(other than
.BR EINTR ).
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGPIPE
Ignored.
.PP
Other signals have their default action;
.I /etc/rc.d/35\-telnetd stop
sends
.BR SIGTERM .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /bin/login
Default login program.
.TP
.I /var/run/telnetd.pid
Process ID, written in both foreground and background mode.
.TP
.I /etc/rc.d/35\-telnetd
Boot script.
.SH EXAMPLE
Run a test server on port 2323 in the foreground:
.EX
# telnetd \-f \-p 2323
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR login (1),
.BR getty (8),
.BR init (8),
.BR openpty (3),
.BR pty (7)
.SH STANDARDS
RFC 854 (TELNET protocol), with the ECHO (RFC 857), SUPPRESS-GO-AHEAD
(RFC 858) and NAWS (RFC 1073) options offered but only partly honoured.
.SH BUGS
Traffic is unencrypted, including passwords.
.PP
A TELNET command split across two reads from the network is not recognised,
and its bytes reach the pty as data.
.PP
When a read from the pty contains 0xff bytes, the doubled output can
exceed the 1024-byte buffer and the excess is dropped.
.PP
.B telnetd
writes no utmp or wtmp records, and a session's logout is not recorded.
.PP
The
.B \-p
argument is converted with
.BR atoi (3)
and not checked.
